        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "where am is the mass, or inertia, matrix, b is the aerodynamic damping matrix, c is the aerodynamic stiffness matrix, d is the structural damping and e is the stiffness matrix. Now, considering the availability of modal data and assuming that the measured damping is dependent solely on the structure itself, d can be built from the uncoupled modal damping assumption in [52]:"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 6,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 231.004, 612.801, 530.764, 626.756 ],
        "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "d = ϕ−Tdnϕ−1 for dn = 2ζnωnan (38)"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 7,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 64.512, 583.511, 530.767, 607.47 ],
        "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "where the subscript n identifies the uncoupled matrix, ωn the natural frequency, ζn the damping ratio and ϕ the mode shape. Hence, Equations (36) and (38) can be combined to assemble Equation (37). Given full knowledge of the wing"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
