    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "amq¨ + dq˙ + eq˙ = 0 (41)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 27,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 64.512, 271.217, 530.763, 378.862 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The natural frequencies can then be easily extracted through eigenanalysis. Hence, by having a set of experimental ωn it is possible to define the EI and GJ of the equivalent system by minimising its squared difference to the experimental ωn. Thus, by using this and the identified ζn for Equation (38), an aeroelastic model can be defined starting from experimental data. To study the system stability, the eigenanalysis of Equation (41) can be solved iteratively with the well-known p-k method [53] to find the divergence and flutter onset speeds. The p-k method is based on the hypothesis that pure harmonic aerodynamics can be used as a good approximation for lightly damped harmonic motions. This allows the computation of the aerodynamic transfer matrix at a complex frequency p = δ ± jk, such that p ≈ jk. In simple terms, the real part, i.e. the damping, is neglected. A widely accepted workflow of the p-k method [49] can be summarised as follow:"
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 28,
    "level" : "5",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 76.965, 205.233, 449.677, 263.296 ],
    "numbering style" : "arabic numbers",
    "number of list items" : 4,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 76.965, 244.686, 370.415, 263.296 ],
      "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "font size" : 9.963,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "1. Initiate an estimation, usually the still air value, of p, said p0 = δ ± jk0",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 76.965, 237.146, 259.005, 250.145 ],
      "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "font size" : 9.963,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "2. Evaluate the aerodynamics, in our case Mθ˙",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 76.965, 218.384, 449.677, 236.994 ],
      "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "font size" : 9.963,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "3. Solve the eigenvalue (λ) problem for Equation (37) and obtain a new set of λ, p1 = δ ± jk1",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 76.965, 205.233, 241.735, 223.843 ],
      "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "font size" : 9.963,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "4. Iterate between 2 and 3 until kn ≈ kn−1",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 29,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 64.512, 144.093, 530.764, 203.918 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "From the λ obtained after convergence, it is possible to build ωn, ζn, real(λ) and imag(λ) vs air-speed (U∞) plots, which can be used to graphically portray divergence or flutter speed, whichever is detected first. Particularly, critical speeds are identified for ζn approaching zero or for real(λ) zero crossings, since both cases are interpreted as instability in the system. Particularly, for flutter, only the real(λ) zero crossing condition needs to be satisfied, while for divergence, imag(λ) must be zero."
  }, {
